WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.318 --> 00:00:10.360
There is nothing quite as breathtaking as a pod of humpback whales swimming in the open ocean.

2
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:17.203
They call to each other with haunting songs, leap majestically into the air, and crash back into the

3
00:00:17.263 --> 00:00:20.324
waves, slipping effortlessly into the watery depths.

4
00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:25.146
Many people enjoy observing whales for their beauty, power, and grace.

5
00:00:25.906 --> 00:00:31.713
But not everyone knows that whales are more closely related to humans than to their fish neighbors.

6
00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:39.536
Humans and whales actually have a lot in common because both are mammals: creatures that are warm-blooded, breathe

7
00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:41.757
air, and give birth to live young.

8
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:49.540
Scientists studying evolution believe that, once upon a time, the ancestors of both people and whales walked on

9
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.321
all fours near the water's edge.

10
00:00:52.621 --> 00:00:58.363
The idea that the ancestors of whales lived on land was once a big problem for biologists.

11
00:00:59.044 --> 00:01:05.741
Even now, some religious scholars think the differences between marine mammals and land dwellers are too great to

12
00:01:05.761 --> 00:01:07.963
be explained by the theory of evolution.

13
00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:17.251
But recent developments in DNA research and fossil analysis have convinced the scientific community that the physical similarities

14
00:01:17.331 --> 00:01:19.333
in the fossil record cannot be ignored.

15
00:01:20.093 --> 00:01:26.639
For example, the bone structure of a whale's flipper greatly resembles the jointed arrangement of bones in a

16
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:27.340
human hand.

17
00:01:28.368 --> 00:01:34.572
Whales also have hip bones, and some specimens even have tiny leg bones—where their hind legs used to

18
00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:37.414
be—contained completely within their bodies.

19
00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:44.820
But researchers have only recently been able to compile this evidence and to understand the amazing changes this

20
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.701
animal has undergone over time.

21
00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:55.007
Over the past few decades, scientists have uncovered the fossils of hundreds of long-dead specimens that have proven

22
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:59.553
to be some of the "missing links" between whales and their land-dwelling ancestors.

23
00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:07.777
With these fossils, scientists are trying to determine how small, furry land animals evolved into the dozens of

24
00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:09.977
species of whales we know today.

25
00:02:11.038 --> 00:02:17.220
Scientists believe that life on planet Earth began in the oceans, over three billion years ago.

26
00:02:18.121 --> 00:02:26.317
Slowly, some organisms began to adapt to dry habitats, until the whole planet—the land and the water—was teeming

27
00:02:26.357 --> 00:02:26.877
with life.

28
00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:32.042
By around 65 million years ago, dinosaurs dominated the landscape.

29
00:02:32.922 --> 00:02:39.208
One popular theory states that the earth changed dramatically when an asteroid slammed into it, throwing up a

30
00:02:39.268 --> 00:02:40.268
massive dust cloud.

31
00:02:41.009 --> 00:02:46.594
This cloud cooled the planet, causing the extinction of dinosaurs and many other plants and animals.

32
00:02:47.595 --> 00:02:51.378
The surviving animals could now take over the newly abandoned territories.

33
00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:56.513
Within a few million years, mammals of all shapes and sizes were flourishing.

34
00:02:57.313 --> 00:03:03.255
One of these survivors evolved into a hoofed animal about the size of a fox, which experts named

35
00:03:03.495 --> 00:03:09.977
Indohyus. This whale ancestor probably took its first steps into the water to avoid predators.

36
00:03:10.917 --> 00:03:16.399
Indohyus didn't look like a whale, but the structure of its bones and teeth was similar to that

37
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:17.339
of modern whales.

38
00:03:18.179 --> 00:03:24.442
Within eight million years, a descendant of this creature had developed an improved ability to hunt in shallow

39
00:03:24.482 --> 00:03:33.646
water. When paleontologists working in Pakistan in 1979 found a fossilized skull of this wolf-sized animal, they named

40
00:03:33.706 --> 00:03:35.887
it Pakicetus, for "Pakistan whale."

41
00:03:36.547 --> 00:03:42.489
The Pakicetus skull had peculiarities in the bones of its ear that exist today only in whales.

42
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.352
Another species was soon unearthed, thought to be around 49 million years old.

43
00:03:49.064 --> 00:03:55.067
The body of Ambulocetus, or "walking whale," shared underwater adaptations with Pakicetus.

44
00:03:55.887 --> 00:04:02.270
Still designed to prowl both the shore and the nearby shallow waters, Ambulocetus may have had help from

45
00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.591
webbed feet, short but powerful limbs, and a strong tail.

46
00:04:06.251 --> 00:04:11.534
Rodhocetus, another intermediary species, appeared around 42 million years ago.

47
00:04:11.554 --> 00:04:16.356
It was the size of a sea lion and could have looked like a crocodile with fur.

48
00:04:17.264 --> 00:04:22.746
By the time of Rodhocetus, the ancient ancestors of whales were spending most of their time in the

49
00:04:22.787 --> 00:04:28.909
water, but they still moved the same way that dogs do today: they basically trotted through water.

50
00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:37.893
Eventually, the same forces that shaped fish—those forces involved in underwater movement—began to impact these mammals.

51
00:04:38.713 --> 00:04:41.975
This is why both whales and fishes have streamlined bodies.

52
00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:48.901
They use them differently, however: Whales swim by moving their tails up and down, while fish swim by

53
00:04:48.981 --> 00:04:50.862
moving their tails from side to side.

54
00:04:51.602 --> 00:04:56.743
Another adaptation developing during the time of Rodhocetus was a new way to hear.

55
00:04:57.404 --> 00:04:59.784
Visibility was often limited in the water.

56
00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:06.526
As a result, these creatures began to depend more on sound than sight to find prey and avoid

57
00:05:06.566 --> 00:05:12.128
predators. The external ear openings of most land animals became less useful.

58
00:05:12.928 --> 00:05:18.167
Instead, the ancestors of whales began to use their lower jaws to focus sounds.

59
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:25.191
The vibrations were then transmitted through their skulls to the auditory canal, a tube inside the ear.

60
00:05:26.231 --> 00:05:30.274
The next known whale ancestors appeared forty million years ago.

61
00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:35.237
Further adaptations in body structure had made them more efficient in the water.

62
00:05:35.937 --> 00:05:42.277
Dorudon and Basilosaurus had lost almost all traces of their hind legs, and the ends of their tails

63
00:05:42.377 --> 00:05:44.378
flared out into horizontal flukes.

64
00:05:45.219 --> 00:05:48.902
Most of their fur had disappeared, making them faster swimmers.

65
00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:55.086
The fronts of their skulls had grown long, and their nostrils had become blowholes at the tops of

66
00:05:55.126 --> 00:05:58.548
their heads, allowing them to breathe easily while swimming.

67
00:05:59.229 --> 00:06:05.973
Through this gradual process, a species that resembled a modern whale had appeared by around 35 million years

68
00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:14.781
ago. The adaptations, however, continued, as whales evolved into two separate groups: toothed whales and baleen whales.

69
00:06:15.422 --> 00:06:19.663
The toothed whales include sperm whales, orcas, dolphins, and porpoises.

70
00:06:20.323 --> 00:06:25.984
Toothed whales developed echolocation, a way to use sound waves to explore their surroundings.

71
00:06:28.045 --> 00:06:34.787
They produce whistles and clicks of different frequencies, and by listening for echoes bouncing off objects, they can

72
00:06:34.847 --> 00:06:38.508
identify the locations of other creatures and structures around them.

73
00:06:39.588 --> 00:06:45.230
Baleen whales are larger than toothed whales and include humpback, blue, gray, and mink whales.

74
00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:52.812
Scientists think baleen whales lost the ability to use echolocation when they stopped chasing after large fish and

75
00:06:52.932 --> 00:06:58.873
other mammals and started eating tiny fish and plankton, which swim in large, slow-moving schools.

76
00:06:59.673 --> 00:07:06.275
The teeth of these whales transformed into baleen—long, thick curtains of hair-like material that filter food from the

77
00:07:06.315 --> 00:07:14.314
water. Although they cannot echolocate, many baleen whales sing complicated songs that can be heard for miles, and

78
00:07:14.374 --> 00:07:18.496
are being studied by experts to try to decipher their mysterious language.

79
00:07:19.716 --> 00:07:25.038
Evolutionary biologists continue to study the unique characteristics of these marine mammals.

80
00:07:25.859 --> 00:07:32.101
Every year, more pieces of the puzzle of life fall into place, providing clues about how whales have

81
00:07:32.141 --> 00:07:40.065
adapted to their watery environment and changed over time from small, furry land-dwellers to the majestic animals that

82
00:07:40.105 --> 00:07:42.227
make their home in the oceans today.
